t there's also the  potential for overfitting you can over  fit your neural network with all of  these knobs and bells and whistles to  your training data in a way that it  might not necessarily generalize  as well as you would like to the future  so this is again a really important  reason to cross validate your data and  in some applications and many  applications overfitting is not  necessarily an issue or something you  have to be concerned about because the  path data is very representative of the  future in fact everything I'll see in  the future you know is either an  interpolation or has analogs in my  previous training data and so as long as  I cross validate my model I will be fine  I'll have good high performance and in  fact those are the applications where  neural networks have typically really  you know shined is where you have huge  vast data sets that do generalize very  well to what you expect to see in the  future ok and I've heard a great analogy  before with overfitting professional  athletes in some sense there are certain  applications where you want to overfit  if you haven't you know NFL linebacker  you want them to be over fit for their  job you don't want them to be good at  other other things you want them to be  just super good at what they do and over  fit so maybe they're incredibly you know  the peak of their you know physical  specimen for what they do but maybe they  would have a hard time hiking up a  mountain that my kids could hike up I  don't know but you know there's this  idea that maybe overfitting is not bad  maybe what you want if you're a company  and you're trying to get 1% better  performance because that's millions or  billions of dollars of profit maybe you  want your model to be kind of over fit  as long as your past data is  representative of your future so I'm  abusing this term a little bit  overfitting is generally a bad thing but  there's some scenarios where if your  training data is rich enough and  generalizes 