's really important that we  can understand and tell other humans how  these algorithms are making decisions or  the algorithm should be explaining  itself that's important and then another  area this is kind of near and dear to my  heart is this idea that as an engineer  and as a scientist I'm not just working  with image classification dogs and cats  or speech recognition I work in a world  where we know physics we know that  energy is conserved and mass is  conserved we know that there are  symmetries and all of these interesting  laws that we've you know hundreds of  years of human knowledge we shouldn't be  throwing this away just be  there is a powerful new architecture we  would love to be able to bake in and  incorporate some of that partial  physical knowledge into these neural  networks we'd love to be able to take  data and learn new physics and I don't  mean this actually in just neural  networks this is true for machine  learning in general we'd love to be able  to figure out how to incorporate  partially known physics and extract  unknown and new physics maybe there is a  physics of how disease spreads or a  physics of the stock market and we'd  like to be able to understand those the  same way we understand F equals MA  and we'd like to be able to incorporate  our partial knowledge into those models  and in general that's these are  challenges with all machine learning  methods but I think these are actually  particularly challenging and neural  networks but because of how powerful and  expressive and just the incredible  technological advances that have been  made because of neural networks these  are especially important to learn how to  incorporate into neural network  architectures the good news is lots of  great people are working on this you all  should work on this it's really really  vital and pressing especially if we're  gonna have you know self-driving cars  and autonomy and robots and you know if  we ever want to get towards you know  from